# Source: Slides "ICT3112 - 5 - Conditional Rule", worked example.
id: conditional-slides
title: The conditional rule with a precondition
topic: Conditional
level: 2
source: Slides 5, Conditional Rule
tags: conditional, wp, consequence

task:
  Prove the following triple, first with the rule IF and then with the
  weakest precondition of the conditional.

pre: x + y = 0
post: x = y + 1

program:
  {P}
  {R3}
  if (x = y) then {
    {R1}
    x := y + 1        // then
  } else {
    {R2}
    x := 1 - x        // else
  }
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: The two premises of the rule IF
rule: IF
instance: IF
  {x + y = 0 && x = y} x := y + 1 {x = y + 1}
  {x + y = 0 && !(x = y)} x := 1 - x {x = y + 1}
  ---
  {x + y = 0} if x = y then x := y + 1 else x := 1 - x {x = y + 1}
text:
  $P$ is $x + y = 0$ and $B$ is $x = y$. We now prove each premise.

step: The then branch
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: then
calc:
  wp(x := y + 1, Q)
  = (x = y + 1)[x <- y + 1]
  = y + 1 = y + 1
  = true
set: R1 = true
text:
  Whatever the state, assigning $y + 1$ to $x$ makes $x = y + 1$ true.

step: The else branch
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: else
calc:
  wp(x := 1 - x, Q)
  = (x = y + 1)[x <- 1 - x]
  = 1 - x = y + 1
  = x = -y                    // subtract 1 and change the signs
set: R2 = x = -y
vc: x + y = 0 && !(x = y) ==> x = -y
why:
  $x + y = 0$ is the same as $x = -y$, so the implication holds.

step: The weakest precondition of the conditional
rule: WP, CONSEQUENCE
calc:
  wp(if (x = y) then x := y + 1 else x := 1 - x, Q)
  = (x = y ==> true) && (!(x = y) ==> 1 - x = y + 1)
set: R3 = (x = y ==> true) && (x != y ==> 1 - x = y + 1)
vc: x + y = 0 ==> (x = y ==> true) && (x != y ==> 1 - x = y + 1)
why:
  The first implication is true since its right side is $true$. For the
  second, assume $x + y = 0$ and $x != y$. From $x = -y$ we get
  $1 - x = 1 + y$, which is $y + 1$.
result: The triple is valid.
